Day 19 — Adopting Infrastructure as Code (IaC)
 Overview

Today I shifted from writing code to thinking about **strategy and real-world adoption of Infrastructure as Code (IaC)**.

Instead of just building Terraform modules, I:

 Reviewed a **real client project**
 Identified current infrastructure gaps
 Created a **4-phase IaC adoption plan**
 Practiced **`terraform import`**
 Discovered **real infrastructure drift**


Project Structure

```bash
day19/
├── import-practice/
│   └── main.tf        # Terraform import practice
└── README.md
```



 Current State Assessment

This is based on a **real SaaS project migrating from Render → AWS**.

 Infrastructure Status

| Area            | Current Situation                     |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Provisioning    | Fully manual (Render dashboard)       |
| Version Control | None for infrastructure               |
| Audit Trail     | Not available                         |
| Secrets         | Stored in `.env.local` on one machine |
| Team Access     | Single developer                      |
| Drift           | Yes — docs ≠ actual setup             |
 **IaC Maturity Level: 0 (No automation at all)**



Key Risks Identified

*  In-memory state (`Map`) → will break in multi-instance AWS setup
*  No secrets management → single point of failure
*  No reproducibility → cannot rebuild infrastructure
*  No audit logs → no visibility into changes



IaC Adoption Plan (4 Phases)

Phase 1 — Foundation (Weeks 1–2)

| Goal    | Details                                 |
| ------- | --------------------------------------- |
| What    | Create S3 backend + DynamoDB lock table |
| Risk    | None (no existing infra touched)        |
| Outcome | First Terraform success                 |

Success:

`terraform apply` works
`terraform plan` shows **No changes**



Phase 2 — Import Existing Infrastructure (Weeks 3–4)

| Task     | Description                                 |
| -------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Import   | Bring existing AWS resources into Terraform |
| Fix Code | Replace in-memory state with Redis          |
| Add      | Docker + CI/CD                              |

Success:

 No changes after `terraform plan`
App builds successfully


Phase 3 — Team Practices (Weeks 5–6)

| Practice               | Why                          |
| ---------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| PR-based changes       | Prevent direct console edits |
| `terraform plan` in PR | Visibility before apply      |
| CI validation          | Catch errors early           |
| State locking          | Prevent conflicts            |


 Phase 4 — Production (Weeks 7–8)

| Outcome            | Value           |
| ------------------ | --------------- |
| Auto deployments   | Faster releases |
| Monitoring         | Visibility      |
| Alerts             | Cost + failures |
| Migration complete | Render → AWS    |



Business Value of IaC

| Problem                | IaC Solution          | Result                  |
| ---------------------- | --------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Lost job data          | Redis shared state    | 100% reliability        |
| Secrets on one machine | AWS Secrets Manager   | No single failure point |
| No audit logs          | Git-based changes     | Full traceability       |
| No dev env             | Separate environments | Safe testing            |
| Cold starts            | Always-on service     | Faster response         |
| No rollback            | Versioned containers  | Fast recovery           |
| Cannot rebuild infra   | Terraform configs     | Disaster recovery       |
| Manual setup           | Reusable modules      | Saves time              |



Efficiency Gains

| Task                 | Before    | After        |
| -------------------- | --------- | ------------ |
| Environment setup    | 2–3 hours | < 10 minutes |
| Developer onboarding | 1 day     | < 2 hours    |



 Terraform Import Practice

 Step 1 — Define Resources

```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "state_bucket" {
  bucket = "<your-bucket-name>"

  tags = {
    ManagedBy = "terraform"
    Environment = "production"
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "state_locks" {
  name         = "<your-table-name>"
  billing_mode = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key     = "LockID"

  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}
```



### Step 2 — Import Resources

```bash
terraform import aws_s3_bucket.state_bucket <bucket-name>
terraform import aws_dynamodb_table.state_locks <table-name>
```



Step 3 — Drift Detected

```bash
Plan: 0 to add, 2 to change, 0 to destroy
```

#### Problem:

 Tags missing
 Naming mismatch

#### Example:

```diff
+ Environment = "production"
+ ManagedBy   = "terraform"
~ Name        = "Old-Name" -> "new-name"
```

---

Step 4 — Fix Drift

```bash
terraform apply
```

✔ After fix:

```bash
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
```



Key Learning: terraform import

| Does                       | Does NOT                      |
| -------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Adds resource to state     | Generate Terraform code       |
| Lets Terraform track infra | Modify resource automatically |
| Helps detect drift         | Fix misconfigurations         |

 You must **write the `.tf` file yourself**



Terraform Cloud vs S3 Backend

| Feature         | S3 Backend | Terraform Cloud |
| --------------- | ---------- | --------------- |
| State storage   | S3         | Managed         |
| Locking         | DynamoDB   | Built-in        |
| UI              | CLI only   | Web UI          |
| Access control  | IAM        | Role-based      |
| Policies        | ❌          | ✅               |
| Cost estimation | ❌          | ✅               |

For now: **S3 backend = best choice (simple + cheap)**



Chapter 10 Learnings

 Why IaC Adoption Fails

1. Trying to migrate everything at once
2. Ignoring team workflow changes

Key Insight

> IaC is not just code — it changes how teams work.



 Challenges Faced

1. Application Design Issue

In-memory state breaks in AWS multi-instance setup
Fix: Use Redis

2. Secrets Management

Stored locally → unsafe
Fix: AWS Secrets Manager



 3. Drift After Import

 Tags inconsistent
 Fix: `terraform apply`



 4. Adoption Resistance

 Developers used to quick console changes
 Fix: Gradual transition + visible wins



  Final Takeaways

 Start small — don’t migrate everything at once
 First visible success builds trust
 `terraform import` exposes real-world problems
 Drift is normal — Terraform helps fix it
 IaC is **as much about people as it is about code**




